      "directNodeIds": [
        "cnm_152",
        "cnm_202"
      ],
      "explanation": "Because the equation equals zero, factoring lets us separate its solutions into simpler alternatives:\n\\[\nx^2-4y^2=(x-2y)(x+2y)=0.\n\\]\nA product of real numbers is zero exactly when at least one factor is zero. Thus a point lies on the graph exactly when\n\\[\nx-2y=0\\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad x+2y=0.\n\\]\nThese equations are \\(y=x/2\\) and \\(y=-x/2\\), two distinct straight lines through the origin. Every point on either line satisfies the original equation, so these lines form the entire graph. The answer is \\(\\boxed{\\text{C}}\\).",
      "id": "factor-and-interpret",
